{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>For.</b> Because in every sickness there is also forgetting, and this is what he said, that <b>this too is hevel</b>, because there is no remembrance of the wise with the fool, and if he had remembrance for days or a few years in the days to come — all is forgotten. And the harsh evil is that the wise man dies with the fool. And the word <b>already</b>, in its context, is not found in all of Scripture except in this book. And one of the commentators said that it refers to a past matter — just as it stood before him <b>already</b> in the days to come, he said: it would have been fitting to be <b>like already</b>; and the matter is that <b>all will be forgotten</b> in the days to come <b>like</b> the time that <b>already</b> passed. And more correct is that its meaning is <b>like behold</b>."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The word rendered 'already' translates שכבר, which Ibn Ezra discusses as anomalous to Ecclesiastes; the commentator paraphrased is unidentified."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:10.320716697Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}